    desc: "The Old City of Sana'a stacks more than 6,000 tower houses of rammed earth and fired brick, faces rendered in warm earth tones and edged with white qadad gypsum bands, blind windows and calligraphic friezes. Upper floors carry wooden-framed qamariya fanlights of painted stained glass; the topmost room, the mafraj, is a qat-chewing lookout with views over the rooftops. Ground floors once stored grain and sheltered animals, a vertical social diagram a millennium old. UNESCO listed the city in 1986 and placed it on the danger list after 2015 air strikes; Sana'ani builders and qadad masters still repair with traditional mixes where war allows.",
