    sum: "The centrally-worn curved dagger whose hilt material and sheath read tribe, region and standing across Yemen.",
    desc: "Every Yemeni man of standing wears the jambiya, a short dagger with a sharply curved blade hung vertically at the centre of a wide belt over the futa wrap and thobe. Status is spoken through the hilt — hardwood safa, horn, or prized agate (aqiq) polished in workshops near Sana'a — and through sheath mounts and embroidered belts, with regional styles differing from Taiz to Sada. The dagger is ceremonial as much as practical: drawn in dance flourishes at weddings, pledged on in mediation, inherited like a name. While ban on carrying is debated in modern Yemen and rhino-horn hilts were outlawed to protect rhinos, the jambiya market in Sana'a's suqs remains a theatre of craft and identity.",
