    desc: "Before oil, the Gulf summer pearling season (ghaus) sent fleets of dhows to the oyster banks with divers who freedive again and again with nose clips (fitaam) and rope weights, while nahham singers on deck kept rhythm through the day. Pearl merchants' houses, diving syndicates and suqs shaped Muharraq, Doha and Dubai; the aesthetic is one of salt-bleached timber, indigo work clothes, mother-of-pearl inlay, and songs of longing sung at sea. Japanese cultured pearls collapsed the trade by the 1930s, but UNESCO listed Bahrain's pearling testaments in 2012 and the restored Pearling Path now walks visitors through merchant houses and diver quarters.",
