    sum: "Dense red-on-black cross-stitch panels that once announced a woman's village and now carry Palestinian identity worldwide.",
    desc: "Tatreez is the cross-stitch (fallahi stitch) tradition worked in counted geometric medallions, classically in madder reds on indigo-black handwoven linen, covering the side panels, chest and sleeves of rural thobes. Stitch patterns encoded place and status — a Ramallah moon, cypress trees, wedding grades — so a dress could be read like a map of villages. After 1948 the craft became a portable national emblem and was re-styled with flag colors and political motifs; UNESCO inscribed Palestinian embroidery on the intangible heritage list in 2023. Revivalists like Wafa Ghnaim teach pattern literacy online, and tatreez now appears on sneakers, gallery walls and protest banners alike.",
