    p: "rural headwear turned global symbol since the 1930s", era: "living symbol",
    sum: "A woven cotton headcloth whose fishnet, olive-leaf and fence-line motifs became the most legible political textile on earth.",
    desc: "The keffiyeh began as practical Levant rural headwear, but its black-on-white herringbone mesh became an icon through the 1936 revolt and Yasser Arafat's carefully draped style. Its three pattern zones are read symbolically: the fishnet as seafarer nets, the bold central braid as olive leaves, the fence-line border as thorned walls. Once woven across the Levant, mass production moved abroad; the Hirbawi factory in Hebron is celebrated as the last Palestinian keffiyeh loom, running cotton through mechanical jacquards in seasonal colorways. Solidarity wear worldwide now spans scarves, graduation stoles and murals, making the hatta a living subcultural uniform.",
