    sum: "Introverted townhouses folded around a fountain courtyard, painted with gilded ajami woodwork under citrus shade.",
    desc: "The Levantine elite house turns its back on the street: plain ablaq walls open into a paved courtyard (wast ad-dar) with a central fountain, fruit trees and raised liwan seats, while the best reception room — the qaa — stacks marble pavement, fountains and polychrome ajami wood paneling gilded with botanical scenes. Damascus and Aleppo merchants built whole quarters of these, with separate family and guest wings and seasons' rooms oriented to sun and shade. Many slid into tenements in the 20th century, then into boutique hotels and museums after restorations; UNESCO's Damascus listing protects a surviving ensemble, and ajami masters still gild new panels for their interiors.",
