    p: "ancient Sumerian lineage; surviving today", era: "living ancient form",
    sum: "Cathedral-scale halls of bundled reeds arched into parabolic ribs — Sumerian architecture still standing in water.",
    desc: "The mudhif is the tribal guesthouse of the Ma'dan (Marsh Arabs): thick bundles of giant kamas reeds bent over and tied into a series of parabolic arches, clad in woven reed mats to make a hall six meters or more high and up to thirty long, with no wood, nails or mud. Light filters through the weave in gold stripes; ceremony, dispute-settlement and hospitality happen beneath. The form descends directly from Sumerian reed construction of five thousand years ago. Saddam's drainage destroyed the marshes in the 1990s; reflooding after 2003 brought communities and mudhifs back, and UNESCO listed the Ahwar of southern Iraq in 2016.",
