    sum: "Baghdad's ornamented sung poetry over santur and joza — an Arab-Persian-Turkish crossroads proclaimed by UNESCO.",
    desc: "The Iraqi maqam is Baghdad's classical song form: a solo qari al-maqam delivers dense, ornamented verses across linked modal movements, framed by the algha ensemble of santur hammered dulcimer, joza spike fiddle, tabla and daff. Its repertory of around thirty? — two dozen? — dozens of maqamat absorbs Persian dastgah colors, Ottoman forms and local Baghdadi Jewish and Muslim poets, making it the sound of the city's cafés along the Tigris. The form was proclaimed a UNESCO masterpiece in 2003, and modern masters like Hamid al-Saadi and the legacy of Muhammad al-Qubbanchi keep it alive through recordings and the reopening of maqam evenings after decades of war.",
    col: [{ h: "#1f3a4d", n: "tigris night blue" }, { h: "#b58a3c", n: "santur brass-gold" }, { h: "#7a1f2b", n: "cafe carpet red" }, { h: "#c8b78e", n: "old paper cream" }, { h: "#3c2f2a", n: "aged wood brown" }],
