    p: "documented medieval onward", era: "living craft",
    sum: "Cylinder-stamped green blocks of olive and laurel berry oil soap, aged in towers like stacked coins of the Silk Road.",
    desc: "Aleppo's soap masters boil olive oil into a green gel, finish with laurel berry oil for the percentage stamped on each block, pour the mass onto warehouse floors, and stamp rounds with family seals before stacking them in honeycomb towers to age nine months to a year — the outside oxidizing gold while the cut stays green. The city's souqs sold blocks whose recipes some historians trace back a millennium and credit (contentiously) as ancestors of Castile and Marseille soaps. War devastated the souq quarter and scattered masters to Gaziantep and Beirut, where revival workshops now stamp blocks with the old geometry: two concentric circles around a calligraphic name.",
