    desc: "Tyrian purple is drawn from the hypobranchial glands of Murex brandaris and Hexaplex trunculus: glands salted, simmered for days in leaded vats, then aired so the foul reek gives way to a dye that shifts from crimson to violet and never fades — one gram needing thousands of snails. Phoenician coastal mounds of crushed shells at Sidon and Tyre mark the industry that gave the sea-snail its name (purple = phoinix) and priced purple cloth above gold, restricted by Roman sumptuary law to emperors. Byzantine emperors were 'born in the purple' chambers hung with it; the craft died with Constantinople in 1453, and modern chemists like Paul Friedlander and dye revivalists have recreated it from Pliny's recipe.",
