    sum: "Golden meleke limestone that clads every Jerusalem facade by law, glowing amber at dusk across the city.",
    desc: "Jerusalem stone — mostly meleke and mizzi limestones quarried around the city — has built the walls, terraces and houses of Jerusalem for three millennia, its warm gold responding to light like no plaster can. A 1918 British Mandate ordinance required new buildings to face stone, a rule Israeli planning kept, so the whole city reads as one luminous material from the Old City walls to Mamilla and the Knesset. Surface finish is a dialect: bossed ancient ashlars, chiseled striations, smooth honed modern panels. Architecturally it binds eras — Herodian courses, Mamluk ablaq, Mandate neo-classicism and Safdie's Mamilla — all speaking the same sand-toned stone.",
