    sum: "Miles of alabaster low relief narrating royal hunts and wars beneath lamassu colossi — the first great visual epics.",
    desc: "Neo-Assyrian kings walled their palaces with continuous gypsum-alabaster (Mosul marble) orthostats carved in low relief: Ashurnasirpal II hunting lions at Nimrud, Sennacherib's Lachish campaign winding across rooms at Nineveh, Ashurbanipal's dying lioness twisting in the British Museum's most admired panels. Winged lamassu — bulls and lions with human heads — guarded gateways, written with cuneiform annals across their bodies so text and image fuse. The carving's grammar of muscle, profile and ranked procession shaped Mesopotamian visual culture and stunned 19th-century Europe when Layard and Botta excavated the mounds. Islamic State destruction in 2015-2016 gutted Nimrud and Nineveh galleries, making surviving slabs doubly precious.",
