    desc: "On Syria's limestone massif, olive-oil wealth built some 40 villages of fine ashlar houses, colonnaded streets, bathhouses, monasteries and martyria between the 1st and 7th centuries, then trade collapsed and everyone left — leaving sites like Serjilla and al-Bara standing in open fields with no medieval or Ottoman overburden. The aesthetic is prose-perfect rural antiquity: golden limestone catching olive-grove light, roofs long gone but walls, door lintels and carved capitals intact. UNESCO inscribed them as the Ancient Villages of Northern Syria in 2011; the war closed access and looting and shellfire have since damaged sites like Brad and Qalblozeh.",
